<h1 class="text-darkgreen">Best Value Colleges: Earnings vs. Debt</h1>

<p class="text-gray-700 pb-4 max-w-3xl">
  A college is a good investment when its graduates earn well and owe little.
  This list ranks every college in the College Scorecard by what students pay or borrow
  against what they earn 10 years later. Pick a ranking below to sort by yearly cost,
  student debt or graduates' debt, from the best value to the worst.
</p>

<section>
  <%= form_for @conn, Routes.college_path(@conn, :rank), [method: :get], fn f -> %>
    <%= select f, :rank, college_rank_views(), onchange: "this.form.submit()", class: "form-input pl-3 pr-0 sm:text-sm sm:leading-5 flex-grow" %>
    <%= select f, :count, [100, 250, 500, 1000], onchange: "this.form.submit()", class: "form-input pl-3 pr-8 sm:text-sm sm:leading-5 flex-grow" %>
  <% end %>


</section>

<table class="md:block xl:table pt-8">
  <thead class="md:absolute md:-top-9999 md:-left-9999">
    <tr>
      <.header label="College Name" tooltip_key="name" />
      <.header label="Admissions Rate" tooltip_key="admissions" />
      <.header label="Avg SAT" tooltip_key="sat" />
      <.header label={humanize_header(List.first(@ranks).cost_field)} tooltip="The cost metric for this ranking view — may be debt, tuition, or yearly cost depending on the ranking selected" />
      <.header label={humanize_header(List.first(@ranks).payoff_field)} tooltip="The earnings metric for this ranking view — may be 1-year, 2-year, or 10-year earnings depending on the ranking selected" />
      <.header label="Difference" tooltip_key="difference" />
      <.header label="Completion" tooltip_key="completion" />
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody class="md:block">

  <%= for rank <- @ranks do %>
    <tr class="md:block odd:bg-gray-50">
      <td class="px-4 py-3 college-field md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-wrap border-b "><%= link rank.college_name, to: Routes.college_path(@conn, :show, rank.unit_id, rank.college_name), class: "no-underline text-green" %> </td>
      <td class="px-4 py-3 admissions-field md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-wrap border-b "><%= percent(rank.admissions) %></td>
      <td class="px-4 py-3 sat-field md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-wrap border-b"><%= sat(rank.sat_avg)%></td>
      <td class="px-4 py-3 cost-field md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-wrap border-b "><%= cash(rank.cost) %></td>
      <td class="px-4 py-3 payoff-field md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-wrap border-b "><%= cash(rank.payoff) %></td>
      <.diff_cell value={rank.diff} />
      <td class="px-4 py-3 md:border-none md:relative md:block xl:text-sm whitespace-nowrap border-b">
        <.completion_badge rate={rank.fouryear_200_completion} />
      </td>
    </tr>
  <% end %>
  </tbody>
</table>

<section class="pt-12 text-gray-700 space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="text-xl text-darkgreen">How we rank colleges</h2>
  <p>
    Each ranking subtracts a cost from an earnings figure. The difference column is that gap:
    the larger it is, the better the return on the money spent or borrowed.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
    <li><strong>Yearly cost to earnings.</strong> Median earnings 10 years after enrolling, minus one year's full cost of attendance. This is the price before financial aid. Grants and scholarships lower it for most families, so the <a href="/calculator" class="text-green">ROI calculator</a> uses net price for your income instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Debt to 10-year earnings.</strong> The same earnings, minus the median student debt of everyone who borrowed, including students who left without a degree.</li>
    <li><strong>Graduated debt to 10-year earnings.</strong> The same, counting only the debt of students who finished.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Earnings alone can flatter a college whose students rarely finish, so the completion column
    shows the graduation rate: the share of students who complete within twice the normal time.
    Read a high return next to a low graduation rate with care.
  </p>
  <h2 class="text-xl text-darkgreen pt-4">Where the numbers come from</h2>
  <p>
    All data comes from the U.S. Department of Education's College Scorecard. Earnings and debt
    are medians for students who received federal financial aid. To compare the same major across
    schools, see <a href="/fields/rank" class="text-green">majors ranked by earnings vs. debt</a>.
  </p>
</section>
